  "query_preview": "..\n\nUpper-level northwest confluent flow will support subsidence and a \ndrying trend through tomorrow. Low clouds should dissipate by early\nevening, and it appears that surface dew pts will lower enough to \nhelp mitigate fog potential tonight. An upper-level ridge axis \nextending north into central Canada will fold over and arrive over \nthe central Great Lakes Thursday evening into Thursday night. \n\nThe forecast then gets complicated Friday and into the Weekend. \nThere is a series over upper-level disturbances that will \nconsolidate over the Northern Plains. How much amplification takes \nplace varies amongst the medium-range models. Deeper and faster \namplification would support a deepening 500 MB trough/low developing \nover Iowa/southern Minnesota on Friday and diving southeast Friday \nnight, shifting the bulk of showers into the Western Ohio Valley. \nSoutheast Michigan has better shot at precipitation with the weak \ncold front dropping south from the northern Great Lakes during the \nsecond half of the weekend. Either way, NBM pops are currently \nrunning around 30-50 percent. Modest instability supports, at best, \njust a slight chance of thunderstorms. \n\nTemperatures through forecast period look to remain around normal, \nnot deviating much more than +/- 5 degrees. Friday looks to feature \nthe warmest max temperatures, per Euro ensembles, reaching the upper \n80s to near 90 degrees.",
